1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,951
Five words.

2
00:00:00,951 --> 00:00:04,013
In five seconds, they become a real Python list.

3
00:00:04,013 --> 00:00:07,522
No copy and paste, no typing the same thing five times.

4
00:00:07,522 --> 00:00:10,137
One cursor became five, and I typed once.

5
00:00:10,137 --> 00:00:17,026
In this video, you'll learn the V S Code shortcuts that do this kind of thing every day, and save you hours.

6
00:00:17,726 --> 00:00:22,918
Every shortcut here is shown for Windows and Linux, and for Mac, side by side.

7
00:00:22,918 --> 00:00:25,648
And every result you see, I ran for real.

8
00:00:25,648 --> 00:00:26,247
Let's go.

9
00:00:26,947 --> 00:00:28,196
Here's the difference.

10
00:00:28,196 --> 00:00:34,950
With the mouse, you scroll to find a file, you drag to select, and you fix the same word in five places, one at a time.

11
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:42,102
With the keyboard, you jump straight to any file or line, you move whole lines with one key, and you edit many places at once.

12
00:00:42,802 --> 00:00:43,735
Here's the plan.

13
00:00:43,735 --> 00:00:47,522
First, moving around: files, lines, the sidebar and the terminal.

14
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:48,979
Then editing whole lines.

15
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:51,485
Then the real superpower, multiple cursors.

16
00:00:51,485 --> 00:00:56,614
Then the rename shortcut, the mistake that trips up almost everyone, and a few pro tips.

17
00:00:57,314 --> 00:00:59,205
One quick rule before we start.

18
00:00:59,205 --> 00:01:03,233
On a Mac, Control usually becomes Command, and Alt becomes Option.

19
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:06,773
I'll show both on the right side of the screen every time.

20
00:01:06,773 --> 00:01:11,168
And on Linux, a couple of shortcuts are different, I'll point those out.

21
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:12,596
Shortcut one.

22
00:01:12,596 --> 00:01:16,180
Control B shows or hides the sidebar, the file list on the left.

23
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:18,701
Hide it, and your code gets the whole screen.

24
00:01:18,701 --> 00:01:20,381
Now, Control P, P for project.

25
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:21,277
It's quick open.

26
00:01:21,277 --> 00:01:25,870
Type a few letters of any file name, like r e p, and the list filters as you type.

27
00:01:25,870 --> 00:01:27,942
Press Enter, and report dot py opens.

28
00:01:27,942 --> 00:01:30,294
No scrolling, no clicking through folders.

29
00:01:30,294 --> 00:01:32,591
Control B again, and the sidebar is gone.

30
00:01:33,291 --> 00:01:34,023
Shortcut two.

31
00:01:34,023 --> 00:01:35,262
Control G, go to line.

32
00:01:35,262 --> 00:01:38,528
When an error says line eight, type eight and press Enter.

33
00:01:38,528 --> 00:01:39,711
You land right there.

34
00:01:39,711 --> 00:01:41,344
On a Mac, it's Control G too.

35
00:01:41,344 --> 00:01:42,414
And shortcut three.

36
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:45,285
Control and the backtick key, the one under Escape.

37
00:01:45,285 --> 00:01:47,763
It opens the terminal right inside V S Code.

38
00:01:47,763 --> 00:01:52,606
Here I run python three report dot py, and see the output, without leaving the editor.

39
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:55,520
Shortcut four is the most important one.

40
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:57,844
Control Shift P opens the Command Palette.

41
00:01:57,844 --> 00:02:00,334
Every single feature in V S Code is in there.

42
00:02:00,334 --> 00:02:01,772
Can't remember a shortcut?

43
00:02:01,772 --> 00:02:06,199
Press this, type what you want, like word wrap, or color theme, and press Enter.

44
00:02:06,199 --> 00:02:11,235
Even better, the list shows the shortcut next to each command, so you learn them as you go.

45
00:02:11,935 --> 00:02:12,746
Now let's edit.

46
00:02:12,746 --> 00:02:14,694
Here's a small shopping cart script.

47
00:02:14,694 --> 00:02:17,291
The subtotal should be printed before the total.

48
00:02:17,291 --> 00:02:18,373
Don't cut and paste.

49
00:02:18,373 --> 00:02:22,107
Put your cursor anywhere on the line, and press Alt and the up arrow.

50
00:02:22,107 --> 00:02:23,405
The whole line moves up.

51
00:02:23,405 --> 00:02:24,487
On a Mac, Option up.

52
00:02:25,187 --> 00:02:26,208
Need one more item?

53
00:02:26,208 --> 00:02:29,751
Put the cursor on the milk line, and press Shift, Alt, down arrow.

54
00:02:29,751 --> 00:02:31,415
The line is copied right below.

55
00:02:31,415 --> 00:02:34,637
Control D selects the word under the cursor, so I type eggs.

56
00:02:34,637 --> 00:02:38,717
Then I click before the price, and Shift End selects to the end of the line.

57
00:02:38,717 --> 00:02:39,737
Type the new price.

58
00:02:39,737 --> 00:02:41,777
Done, without retyping the whole line.

59
00:02:41,777 --> 00:02:44,247
On Linux, copy line is Control Shift Alt down.

60
00:02:44,947 --> 00:02:47,399
This debug line prints the whole dictionary.

61
00:02:47,399 --> 00:02:50,186
Control slash comments it out, so Python skips it.

62
00:02:50,186 --> 00:02:51,858
Press it again, and it's back.

63
00:02:51,858 --> 00:02:53,808
This is perfect for testing things.

64
00:02:53,808 --> 00:02:58,211
And when you're sure you don't need it, Control Shift K deletes the whole line.

65
00:02:58,211 --> 00:02:59,270
No selecting first.

66
00:02:59,970 --> 00:03:02,764
Two bonus moves that need no selection at all.

67
00:03:02,764 --> 00:03:08,231
With nothing selected, Control C copies the whole line, and Control X cuts the whole line.

68
00:03:08,231 --> 00:03:10,539
Then paste it anywhere with Control V.

69
00:03:10,539 --> 00:03:16,918
It's the fastest way to move a line a long distance, when Alt and the arrows would take too many presses.

70
00:03:17,617 --> 00:03:18,403
Let's run it.

71
00:03:18,403 --> 00:03:22,630
Subtotal seven seventy nine, then the total with tax, eight forty one.

72
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:25,287
The eggs are in, and the debug line is gone.

73
00:03:25,287 --> 00:03:28,186
Four quick shortcuts, and the mouse never moved.

74
00:03:28,886 --> 00:03:29,895
Now the superpower.

75
00:03:29,895 --> 00:03:32,495
I want to rename the variable total, to subtotal.

76
00:03:32,495 --> 00:03:35,255
Put the cursor inside the word, and press Control D.

77
00:03:35,255 --> 00:03:36,317
It selects the word.

78
00:03:36,317 --> 00:03:38,970
Press Control D again, and it adds the next match.

79
00:03:38,970 --> 00:03:39,501
And again.

80
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:40,244
Three cursors.

81
00:03:40,244 --> 00:03:42,633
Now type once, and all three change together.

82
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,323
Look closely.

83
00:03:43,323 --> 00:03:46,666
The word Total inside the text, and Subtotal, were not touched.

84
00:03:46,666 --> 00:03:49,055
That's because we started with just a cursor.

85
00:03:49,755 --> 00:03:52,590
And this is the mistake that trips up almost everyone.

86
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:58,156
If you first select the word by hand, with the mouse or Shift and the arrows, Control D works differently.

87
00:03:58,156 --> 00:04:02,094
It now ignores upper and lower case, and it matches inside other words too.

88
00:04:02,094 --> 00:04:02,409
Watch.

89
00:04:02,409 --> 00:04:04,089
It grabs Total, inside the text.

90
00:04:04,089 --> 00:04:05,507
And total, inside Subtotal.

91
00:04:05,507 --> 00:04:07,502
Type now, and you'd break your output.

92
00:04:07,502 --> 00:04:08,447
The fix is simple.

93
00:04:08,447 --> 00:04:12,910
Press Escape, click once inside the word with no selection, and then press Control D.

94
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:18,105
When a word appears twenty times, pressing Control D twenty times is slow.

95
00:04:18,105 --> 00:04:21,628
Select it once with Control D, then press Control Shift L.

96
00:04:21,628 --> 00:04:23,997
Every match gets a cursor, all at once.

97
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:26,001
Now I'll use the Command Palette.

98
00:04:26,001 --> 00:04:29,828
Control Shift P, type upper, and choose Transform to Uppercase.

99
00:04:29,828 --> 00:04:32,379
Tax becomes a capital TAX, in both places.

100
00:04:32,379 --> 00:04:35,720
In Python, capitals are the usual style for a constant.

101
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:39,002
Same result as before, so nothing broke.

102
00:04:39,002 --> 00:04:42,294
Subtotal seven seventy nine, total eight forty one.

103
00:04:42,294 --> 00:04:47,522
Renaming with multiple cursors is safe, as long as you start from a plain cursor.

104
00:04:48,222 --> 00:04:50,599
Now, the trick from the start of the video.

105
00:04:50,599 --> 00:04:51,815
A plain list of words.

106
00:04:51,815 --> 00:04:54,027
Press Control Alt down arrow four times.

107
00:04:54,027 --> 00:04:55,685
On a Mac, Command Option down.

108
00:04:55,685 --> 00:04:57,952
Now there are five cursors, one per line.

109
00:04:57,952 --> 00:04:59,555
Type four spaces and a quote.

110
00:04:59,555 --> 00:05:05,359
Press End, to jump every cursor to the end of its own line, even though the lines have different lengths.

111
00:05:05,359 --> 00:05:06,741
Type a quote and a comma.

112
00:05:06,741 --> 00:05:08,455
Escape goes back to one cursor.

113
00:05:09,155 --> 00:05:12,171
To finish, Control Shift Enter adds a new line above.

114
00:05:12,171 --> 00:05:14,960
It keeps the indent, so one Backspace removes it.

115
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:16,895
I type items equals, open bracket.

116
00:05:16,895 --> 00:05:18,546
Control G, line six, and End.

117
00:05:18,546 --> 00:05:22,302
Control Enter adds a line below, without breaking the current one.

118
00:05:22,302 --> 00:05:25,034
Backspace removes the indent, close the bracket.

119
00:05:25,034 --> 00:05:27,368
One more Control Enter, and a print line.

120
00:05:27,368 --> 00:05:28,107
Let's run it.

121
00:05:28,807 --> 00:05:29,533
Five items.

122
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:31,708
Apple, bread, milk, eggs, coffee.

123
00:05:31,708 --> 00:05:34,147
A real Python list, built in seconds.

124
00:05:34,147 --> 00:05:37,904
And on Linux, add cursor below is Shift Alt down instead.

125
00:05:38,604 --> 00:05:40,417
Renaming a function is different.

126
00:05:40,417 --> 00:05:43,165
Its name can also appear in comments, and in text.

127
00:05:43,165 --> 00:05:45,362
Put the cursor on the name and press F2.

128
00:05:45,362 --> 00:05:47,945
Type the new name, line total, and press Enter.

129
00:05:47,945 --> 00:05:50,747
V S Code renames the function and every call to it.

130
00:05:50,747 --> 00:05:55,857
But the comment on line one and the text on line six stay the same, because they're not code.

131
00:05:55,857 --> 00:05:59,264
This needs the Python extension, and it works the same on Mac.

132
00:05:59,963 --> 00:06:01,019
And it still runs.

133
00:06:01,019 --> 00:06:02,602
Milk, one point nine eight.

134
00:06:02,602 --> 00:06:06,296
And apple shows three point five nine nine, with lots of nines.

135
00:06:06,296 --> 00:06:07,586
That's not the rename.

136
00:06:07,586 --> 00:06:09,990
It's how computers store decimal numbers.

137
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:13,156
Wrap it in round, if you want a clean three point six.

138
00:06:13,856 --> 00:06:15,502
Two more problems beginners hit.

139
00:06:15,502 --> 00:06:18,329
First, a shortcut does nothing, or does something else.

140
00:06:18,329 --> 00:06:21,466
Your operating system, or another app, may use the same keys.

141
00:06:21,466 --> 00:06:25,271
Open the Keyboard Shortcuts editor, search the command, and pick new keys.

142
00:06:25,271 --> 00:06:27,122
Second, a different keyboard layout.

143
00:06:27,122 --> 00:06:30,155
The backtick key isn't in the same place on every keyboard.

144
00:06:30,155 --> 00:06:35,091
If Control backtick doesn't open the terminal, use the Command Palette, and search for terminal.

145
00:06:35,792 --> 00:06:37,240
Here's your cheat sheet.

146
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:38,689
Control P to open files.

147
00:06:38,689 --> 00:06:40,742
Control Shift P for every command.

148
00:06:40,742 --> 00:06:42,312
Control G to go to a line.

149
00:06:42,312 --> 00:06:44,123
Alt up and down to move lines.

150
00:06:44,123 --> 00:06:45,934
Shift Alt down to copy a line.

151
00:06:45,934 --> 00:06:47,444
Control slash to comment.

152
00:06:47,444 --> 00:06:49,436
Control Shift K to delete a line.

153
00:06:49,436 --> 00:06:52,515
Control D and Control Shift L for multiple cursors.

154
00:06:52,515 --> 00:06:53,542
And F2 to rename.

155
00:06:53,542 --> 00:06:55,896
Pause the video, and take a screenshot.

156
00:06:56,596 --> 00:06:57,251
Pro tip one.

157
00:06:57,251 --> 00:06:59,651
Shift Alt right arrow expands the selection.

158
00:06:59,651 --> 00:07:03,742
Press it again, and it grows from the word, to the text, to the whole line.

159
00:07:03,742 --> 00:07:06,087
On a Mac, it's Control Shift Command right.

160
00:07:06,087 --> 00:07:06,742
Pro tip two.

161
00:07:06,742 --> 00:07:10,614
Alt and click, Option click on a Mac, adds a cursor anywhere you click.

162
00:07:10,614 --> 00:07:13,778
Great when the places you want to edit aren't in a column.

163
00:07:14,478 --> 00:07:15,255
Pro tip three.

164
00:07:15,255 --> 00:07:18,752
Control K, then Control S, opens the Keyboard Shortcuts editor.

165
00:07:18,752 --> 00:07:21,416
Search any command, and you'll see its shortcut.

166
00:07:21,416 --> 00:07:22,692
You can even change it.

167
00:07:22,692 --> 00:07:24,912
And don't try to learn everything today.

168
00:07:24,912 --> 00:07:29,463
Pick two shortcuts this week, use them until they're automatic, then add two more.

169
00:07:30,163 --> 00:07:31,942
Here's how to make these stick.

170
00:07:31,942 --> 00:07:33,320
This week, use only two.

171
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:36,707
Control P to open files, and Alt up and down to move lines.

172
00:07:36,707 --> 00:07:38,946
Next week, add Control D and Control G.

173
00:07:38,946 --> 00:07:41,414
The week after, the Command Palette and F2.

174
00:07:41,414 --> 00:07:45,949
Each time you reach for the mouse, stop, and ask, is there a shortcut for this?

175
00:07:45,949 --> 00:07:47,729
Then check the Command Palette.

176
00:07:48,429 --> 00:07:49,196
Quick recap.

177
00:07:49,196 --> 00:07:52,714
Control P to jump to files, Control G to jump to lines.

178
00:07:52,714 --> 00:07:55,336
Control Shift P when you forget anything.

179
00:07:55,336 --> 00:07:58,470
Alt arrows to move lines, Shift Alt to copy them.

180
00:07:58,470 --> 00:08:02,243
Control D for the next match, starting from a plain cursor.

181
00:08:02,243 --> 00:08:04,289
Control Shift L for all of them.

182
00:08:04,289 --> 00:08:05,824
And F2 to rename safely.

183
00:08:06,524 --> 00:08:12,525
Next on Shortcut Lab, merge hundreds of Excel files into one, with a few lines of Python.

184
00:08:12,525 --> 00:08:14,682
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
